<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place</p><h1 class="enc-title">Radcliffe Line</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Radcliffe Line is the boundary drawn in 1947 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, named after the British lawyer Sir Cyril Radcliffe, who chaired the two boundary commissions. Radcliffe had never been to India and had about five weeks for the task. The award was published on 17 August 1947, two days after independence, and the partition it fixed was accompanied by mass migration and communal violence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/radcliffe-line.jpg" alt="Radcliffe Line" width="320" height="315" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Own work · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Partition_of_India_1947_en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence-act-1947">Indian Independence Act 1947</a> ended British rule on 15 August 1947 and divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> into the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>. Baluchistan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-frontier-province">North-West Frontier Province</a> went wholly to Pakistan, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> (55.7 percent Muslim) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> (54.4 percent) had no overwhelming majority and were themselves partitioned. Proposals to divide Punjab dated from 1908, and Bengal had been partitioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-curzon">Lord Curzon</a> in 1905 and reunited in 1911.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> no line could cleanly separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, Muslims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>, and any division risked cutting roads, railways, irrigation and even individual landholdings. In June 1947 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> appointed Sir Cyril Radcliffe to chair two boundary commissions, one each for Punjab and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Each commission had four members, two nominated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a> and two by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a>. They were told to demarcate contiguous majority areas of Muslims and non-Muslims while taking other factors into account, such as natural boundaries, communications and irrigation. The two sides deadlocked, so the decision was essentially Radcliffe's. The commissioners were all lawyers with no advisers and little time, and Radcliffe, who had not previously visited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, destroyed his papers and left on Independence Day.</p><p>The awards were ready on 9 and 12 August but were handed to the Indian and Pakistani representatives only on 16 August, with two hours to study them, and published on 17 August. Evidence suggests Nehru and Patel may have learned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> award's content in advance, and part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firozpur-district">Ferozepur district</a> was shifted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A Punjab Boundary Force of 50,000 men, less than one soldier to a square mile, could not prevent thousands of killings or protect the refugee columns. Disputes centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, which went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, on Gurdaspur, and on the Chittagong Hill Tracts, whose population was 97 percent non-Muslim but which was awarded to Pakistan. The Punjab part of the line is now part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>–Pakistan border and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> part is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>–India border. Border conflicts led to wars in 1947, 1965 and 1971, and the Kargil conflict of 1999. The line also divided a centuries-old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> pilgrimage and separated people of Punjab of all faiths from each other.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Radcliffe Line” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
